// wb-docker-app: keep containerized services off unattended-upgrades
// (design.md §3.11, issue #5).
//
// Container apps are riskier to auto-upgrade than system packages: a major
// Node-RED / Home Assistant bump can break user flows/configs or migrate data
// forward-incompatibly, and an unexpected restart mid-automation is worse than
// a deferred upgrade. So upgrades happen only on purpose, via an explicit
// `apt upgrade wb-<service>` (whose postinst runs `compose pull` + `up -d`).
//
// We blacklist the shared helper and every service package by the `wb-`
// docker-app naming convention. Patterns are Python regexes anchored at the
// start; we anchor the end with `$` so `wb-docker-app` matches only itself and
// not some unrelated `wb-docker-app-foo`. Service packages are matched by the
// `wb-` prefix shared with the rest of the WB platform, so we intentionally
// scope to the ones that ship a base compose under this helper rather than
// blacklisting all of `wb-*` (which would freeze unrelated WB system packages).
//
// Shipped by the wb-docker-app helper package (one per system): it blacklists
// itself and the in-tree node-red service package. Because the entries are
// end-anchored, a new curated service package whose slug does not already match
// an entry below ships its own matching end-anchored drop-in under
// etc/apt/apt.conf.d/.
Unattended-Upgrade::Package-Blacklist {
	"wb-docker-app$";
	"wb-node-red$";
};
